{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Corroborating evidence from the durable usage record: the failing calls consume NOTHING\n\nNew evidence, now queryable rather than grep-able, thanks to the instrumentation landed by\n[rebar:aec1-76e7-bb95-4c86] (merged as `ff4c56007a`). This is the rendered output of\n`python -m rebar.llm.usage_log summarize .rebar/usage.jsonl` over a real 73-call gate log:\n\n    | op | calls | input | output | cache_read | cache_write | requests | est. cost |\n    | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n    | overlap-judge | 25 | 518523 | 343682 | 66800 | 7216 | 41 | $7.1894 |\n    | plan-review-prerequisite-verifier | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | $0.0000 |\n    | plan-reviewer | 40 | 5574016 | 111988 | 4672448 | 849125 | 231 | $11.7757 |\n    | ticket-digest | 4 | 31094 | 1741 | 0 | 0 | 4 | $0.0438 |\n\n    #### Run shape (loop vs breadth)\n\n    | op | calls | tool_calls | distinct | ratio | max_repeat | request_limit | tool_calls_limit |\n    | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n    | overlap-judge | 25 | 0 | 0 | — | 0 | 125 | 250 |\n    | plan-review-prerequisite-verifier | 4 | 0 | 0 | — | 0 | 125 | 250 |\n    | plan-reviewer | 40 | 400 | 400 | 1.000 | 1 | 125 | 250 |\n    | ticket-digest | 4 | 0 | 0 | — | 0 | 125 | 250 |\n\n### What this adds that the logs could not\n\n**`plan-review-prerequisite-verifier`: 4 calls, `requests=0`, 0 input tokens, 0 output tokens,\n$0.0000.**\n\nEvery other op consumed real tokens. This one consumed **nothing at all across four calls** — the\nrequest was rejected BEFORE any generation occurred. That is independent, durable confirmation of\nthe mechanism proven here: the failure is a pre-response rejection of the REQUEST (Bedrock refusing\nto compile the constrained-decoding grammar), not a generation, truncation, retry-exhaustion or\ncontent problem. A model that had produced anything would show non-zero output tokens.\n\nIt also rules out, from the record alone, three explanations that cost real time to eliminate:\n* **not a loop** — `tool_calls=0`, `max_repeat=0`. Contrast `plan-reviewer` at `400/400 = 1.000`,\n  healthy breadth.\n* **not budget exhaustion** — 0 of 125 requests used; the limits were never approached.\n* **not cost/throttling** — $0.0000 consumed.\n\n### The sibling session's timing fingerprint\n\nThe same instrumentation recorded these four calls at **185.6-185.7s** with `requests=0`. A call\nthat consumes zero tokens yet takes over three minutes is a server-side compile budget expiring —\nmatching Anthropic's documented **180-second grammar-compilation timeout** exactly, and matching the\n27 failures measured here at 180.6-185.9s.\n\n### Note on which failure shape this log shows\n\nThis log comes from a worktree on **newer botocore**, so it shows Variant A (the 185s server-side\n400). On the main checkout's botocore 1.40.61 the same calls fail as Variant B — `0.0s`,\n`Unknown parameter in input: \"outputConfig\"`, client-side — which would also appear here as\n`requests=0` but with a sub-second duration. Duration is the discriminator between the two shapes,\nand it is now on disk.\n\n### Why this matters for the permanent fix\n\nZero token consumption means **every one of these calls is pure waste** — no partial work is\nrecovered, nothing is cached, and the retry that follows repeats it exactly. It also means the fix\nhas a clean success signal to test against: after the fix this op must show non-zero `requests` and\nnon-zero output tokens, because it will actually run."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785959961125889001,"uuid":"bae9a71f-31ae-4cd7-a0ef-91f0ea6c0bfc"}